---
title: Kernel Modules
order: 1
description: lsmod, modprobe, modinfo, rmmod, blacklisting
tags: [kernel, modules, lsmod, modprobe, modinfo, rmmod, blacklist]
---

Kernel modules are loadable drivers and features that extend kernel functionality without recompiling.

@text
Listing Modules

@code bash
# List all loaded modules
lsmod

# List loaded modules sorted by size
lsmod | sort -k2 -n -r

# Check if a specific module is loaded
lsmod | grep nvidia

# Count loaded modules
lsmod | wc -l

@text
Module Information

@code bash
# Show detailed info about a module
modinfo nvidia

# Show only specific fields
modinfo -F description nvidia
modinfo -F depends nvidia
modinfo -F parm nvidia          # Parameters the module accepts
modinfo -F filename nvidia      # Path to .ko file

# Find which module provides a device
# Check device's modalias
cat /sys/class/net/eth0/device/modalias
modprobe --show-depends $(cat /sys/class/net/eth0/device/modalias)

@text
Loading Modules

@code bash
# Load a module
sudo modprobe nvidia

# Load with parameters
sudo modprobe nvidia NVreg_UsePageAttributeTable=1

# Load a module and all dependencies
sudo modprobe snd_hda_intel

# Dry run — show what would be loaded
modprobe --show-depends snd_hda_intel

# Load from a specific file (rarely needed)
sudo insmod /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/kernel/drivers/gpu/drm/nvidia/nvidia.ko

@text
Removing Modules

@code bash
# Remove a module
sudo modprobe -r nvidia

# Remove and all unused dependencies
sudo modprobe -r nvidia

# Force remove (dangerous — may crash system)
sudo rmmod -f nvidia

# Remove fails if module is in use — check users
lsmod | grep nvidia   # look at "Used by" column

@text
Persistent Module Loading

@code bash
# Load modules at boot — create a .conf file in /etc/modules-load.d/
echo "nvidia" | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/nvidia.conf

# Multiple modules in one file
sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/custom.conf << 'EOF'
# Custom modules
nvidia
nvidia_modeset
nvidia_uvm
nvidia_drm
EOF

@text
Module Parameters

@code bash
# View current parameters of a loaded module
systool -v -m nvidia | grep parm
# or
ls /sys/module/nvidia/parameters/
cat /sys/module/nvidia/parameters/NVreg_UsePageAttributeTable

# Set parameters at load time via /etc/modprobe.d/
sudo tee /etc/modprobe.d/nvidia.conf << 'EOF'
options nvidia NVreg_UsePageAttributeTable=1
options nvidia_drm modeset=1
EOF

# Set parameters at boot via kernel command line (GRUB)
# In /etc/default/grub:
# GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT="nvidia-drm.modeset=1"

@text
Blacklisting Modules

Prevent a module from loading automatically.

@code bash
# Blacklist via modprobe config
sudo tee /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-nouveau.conf << 'EOF'
blacklist nouveau
options nouveau modeset=0
EOF

# Blacklist at boot (kernel parameter)
# In /etc/default/grub:
# GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT="modprobe.blacklist=nouveau"

# Verify a module is blacklisted
modprobe --showconfig | grep blacklist | grep nouveau

# After blacklisting, rebuild initramfs if the module was included
sudo mkinitcpio -P

@text
Module Dependencies

@code bash
# Show module dependency tree
modprobe --show-depends nvidia

# View the modules.dep file
less /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/modules.dep

# Regenerate module dependencies (after manual module install)
sudo depmod -a

# Check for a specific module
depmod -n | grep nvidia
